 "text": "**No Monzo fees on card spending abroad; cash withdrawals are fee-free only up to certain limits (then 3%).**\n\n### Card spending\nYou can pay with your Monzo card in any currency, fee-free. Monzo passes on Mastercard’s exchange rate with no markup or foreign transaction fee. This applies on free and paid plans.\n\n### Cash withdrawals (ATMs)\nLimits are on a **rolling 30-day** basis (they reset 30 days after each withdrawal, not by calendar month) and are shared across your personal and joint accounts. After the free allowance, Monzo charges **3%**.\n\n**In the UK and EEA** (EU countries plus Iceland, Liechtenstein and Norway):\n- Free account, and Monzo is **not** your main bank: £400 fee-free, then 3%\n- Free account, and Monzo **is** your main bank: unlimited fee-free\n- Extra, Plus, Premium, Perks and Max: unlimited fee-free\n\n**Outside the EEA:**\n- Free account (whether or not it’s your main bank) and Extra: £200 fee-free, then 3%\n- Plus (legacy plan): £400 fee-free, then 3%\n- Premium (legacy), Perks and Max: £600 fee-free, then 3%\n\n**“Main bank”** (for free accounts, mainly affecting UK/EEA cash limits) generally means at least £500 paid in over a rolling 35 days plus an active Direct Debit, or certain benefits/student loan payments (or a joint account with someone who meets the criteria). You can check your status and remaining allowance in the app under Fees and allowances.\n\n### Other points\n- Some ATMs charge their own operator fee (Monzo doesn’t receive this). Prefer free machines where possible.\n- Always choose to be charged in the **local currency**, not GBP, to avoid poorer dynamic currency conversion rates from the ATM or merchant.\n- Plus and Premium are older plans no longer open to new customers; current paid plans are Extra, Perks and Max.\n\nDetails are from Monzo’s help pages and fee information (current account and Extra/Perks/Max documents, dated around August 2026). Fees can change, so confirm in the Monzo app or on monzo.com before you travel.",
